      'One of Hindi cinema’s most enduring and prolific female composers, Usha Khanna broke barriers when producer Sashadhar Mukherjee launched her at age 18 alongside lyricist Majrooh Sultanpuri in Dil Deke Dekho (1959). Across a four-decade mainstream career from Hum Hindustani (1960), Aao Pyar Karen (1964), Shabnam (1964), Ek Sapera Ek Lutera (1965), Sabak (1973), Hawas (1974), Saajan Bina Suhagan (1978), Dada (1979), and Aap To Aise Na The (1980) to Souten (1983) and Dil Pardesi Ho Gayaa (2003), she balanced Shammi Kapoor-era rock-’n’-roll exuberance with evergreen ghazals, qawwalis, and poignant family melodies ("Chhodo Kal Ki Baatein", "Teri Galiyon Mein", "Madhuban Khushboo Deta Hai", "Dil Ke Tukde Tukde Karke", "Tu Is Tarah Se", "Shaayad Meri Shaadi"). This dossier preserves 158 verified released Hindi (Bollywood) feature films and 796 playable songs. Broader encyclopedic estimates citing "roughly 150 to 250 films" incorporate her regional soundtracks across Malayalam (Moodalmanju, Aadyapapam, Agni Nilaavu, Puthooramputhri Unniyarcha), Gujarati (Dikari Ne Gaai Dore Tyan Jaye, Mann Moti ’Ne Kaach), Bengali (Abichar, Chhinna Samaj), Marathi (Vidhilikhit), Bhojpuri, and Tamil cinema, Doordarshan’s Chandrakanta, non-film albums (Mausam 1985, Prem Pukar 2009), shelved projects (Pahle Paisa Phir Bhagwan, Pattharon Ka Shaher, Salam-E-Mohabbat, Fitarat, Teri Aarzoo), misattributed titles (Lahu Luhan 1991, Kamsin Haseena 1992), and guest co-composers’ tracks on shared OSTs (Kharidar, Bijli, Mehak)—alongside documented inspirations from The McGuire Sisters, Paul Anka, Ivory Joe Hunter, Hava Nagila, Henry Mancini, Ernesto Lecuona, and Ahmed Rushdi.',
