    editorialEssay:
      'Trained on violin from childhood and revered across two decades as Bollywood’s preeminent string arranger for Ilaiyaraaja, Naushad, R. D. Burman, Bappi Lahiri, and Raamlaxman (Maine Pyar Kiya, Hum Aapke Hain Koun..!, Dilwale Dulhania Le Jayenge), Uttam Singh first stepped into film composing as one half of Uttam–Jagdish alongside Jagdish Khanna (Painter Babu 1983, Teesra Kinara 1986, Waaris 1988, Kabrastan 1988, Paanch Fauladi 1989, Clerk 1989, Gawaahi 1989). Yash Chopra’s Dil To Pagal Hai (1997) launched his landmark solo run—redefining 1990s romance with sweeping 100-piece string arrangements, Lata Mangeshkar and Udit Narayan classics ("Dil To Pagal Hai", "Bholi Si Surat", "Are Re Are"), and Asha Bhosle’s "Le Gayi"—followed rapidly by Dushman (1998, "Chithi Na Koi Sandesh"), Hum Tum Pe Marte Hain (1999), Farz (2001), all-time chartbuster Gadar: Ek Prem Katha (2001), Pyaar Diwana Hota Hai (2002), The Hero: Love Story of a Spy (2003), National Award-winning Partition epic Pinjar (2003), Ho Jaata Hai Pyaar (2005), Kachchi Sadak (2006), Rajjo (2013), and Honour Killing (2014). This dossier maintains strictly released Hindi (Bollywood) song soundtracks (19 films, 125 playable tracks): excluding BGM-only Hindi films (Baghban, Taj Mahal: An Eternal Love Story, Hum Tumhare Hain Sanam, Farishtay), music-arranger-only credits, co-composers’ tracks on Teesra Kinara (Shyam Sagar) and Farz (Anu Malik, Aadesh Shrivastava), Sony Music’s Aadesh Shrivastava-credited Dil Ne Phir Yaad Kiya (2001), Mithoon’s 2023 Gadar 2 recreations, TV scores, and Punjabi/Malayalam/Bhojpuri/Marathi features (Chann Pardesi, Mehndi Shagna Di, Prem Poojari, Ek Jind Ek Jaan, Nanak Shah Fakir, Swami Public Ltd., Sajjan Singh Rangroot). Documented origins cover Farid al-Atrash ("Le Gayi" ~ "Ya Habaybi Ya Ghaybin") alongside Punjabi Suhaag wedding folk ("Traditional Shaadi Geet"), Gurbani Shabad Kirtan ("Shabad"), and Amrita Pritam’s "Ajj Aakhaan Waris Shah Nu" ("Waris Shah Nu").',
