    editorialEssay:
      'Born Ramchandra Narhar Chitalkar in Puntamba, Maharashtra and trained in Hindustani classical music before entering Bombay studios as an actor, assistant, and composer under the playback pseudonym "Chitalkar", C. Ramchandra transformed early Hindi cinema by fusing Indian melodic grace with brass swing, Latin rumba, waltz, and rock-and-roll grooves. After early 1940s scores beginning with Sukhi Jeevan (1942), Bhakt Raj (1943), and Safar (1946), his swing blockbuster Shehnai (1947, "Aana Meri Jaan, Meri Jaan, Sunday Ke Sunday"), Purabiya folk hit Nadiya Ke Paar (1948), and Patanga (1949, "Mere Piya Gaye Rangun") ushered in an unmatched 1950s golden run across Nirala (1950), Samadhi (1950, "Gore Gore O Banke Chore"), Sargam (1950), Bhagwan Dada’s immortal musical Albela (1951, "Shola Jo Bhadke", "Bholi Surat Dil Ke Khote"), Anarkali (1953, "Yeh Zindagi Usi Ki Hai", "Jaag Dard-E-Ishq Jaag"), Subah Ka Tara (1954), Azaad (1955), Insaniyat (1955), Kishore Kumar’s rock-and-roll milestone Aasha (1957, "Eena Meena Deeka"), Sharada (1957), Nausherwan-E-Adil (1957), V. Shantaram’s Technicolor classic Navrang (1959), Paigham (1959), Stree (1961), Bahurani (1963), Tasveer (1966), Tulsi Vivah (1971), and Toofani Takkar (1978). This dossier indexes all 97 released Hindi (Bollywood) feature films with verified playable vocal recordings and 730 tracks: excluding regional Tamil/Telugu/Marathi works (Vana Mohini, Vanji Kottai Valipan, Akbar Salim Anarkali, Dhananjay, Sant Nivrutti Dnyandev, Gharkul, Ram Ram Pahune; while retaining the released Hindi bilingual Chhatrapati Shivaji), unreleased/shelved projects (Nazrana 1948/49, Jitne Door Utne Paas), misattributed or compilation/remake credits (Ashirwad 1943, Savdhan 1954, Kalakar 1954, Sindoor 1947, Madam Zapatta 1962, Abhi To Main Jawan Hoon 1989, Shaandaar 2015, Mere Pyare Prime Minister 2019, and pop tracks mis-merged onto Roshni), acting-only films (Pukar, Jailor, Lal Haveli, Tadbir), non-film recordings (including the 1963 live classic "Ae Mere Watan Ke Logon" and devotional/patriotic singles), Stree’s instrumental "Virah Nrutya", nine unuploaded/lost 1942–1948 films lacking surviving playable uploads, and co-composers’ tracks on shared Hindi OSTs (Girls School, Shabistan, Saudagar, Anarkali, Ladki). Documented global influences span Benny Goodman swing ("Aana Meri Jaan"), Edmundo Ros’s "Chico Chico" ("Gore Gore"), Ernesto Lecuona’s "Andalucia" ("Haal Tujhe Apni Duniya Ka"), "Eeny, Meeny, Miny, Moe" / John Gomes & Danny Kaye scat novelty ("Eena Meena Deeka"), and Domenico Modugno’s "Volare" ("Aaja Re Aaja Lage Na Mora Jiya").',
