      'Born Chitragupt Shrivastava in Karmaini (Gopalganj, Bihar) after earning dual postgraduate degrees in Economics and Journalism at Patna University, Chitragupt moved to Bombay in the mid-1940s and assisted S. N. Tripathi before debuting as an independent composer on Talpade Productions’ Fighting Hero (1946), Toofan Queen (1946), and Jadui Ratan (1947). Across his first decade he anchored scores for Homi Wadia and Basant Pictures mythologically and fantasy adventures—highlighted by Bhakt Puran (1952), Alibaba Aur 40 Chor (1954, jointly scored with S. N. Tripathi), and Shiv Bhakta (1955)—before AVM Productions’ blockbuster Bhabhi (1957, "Chal Ud Ja Re Panchhi", "Chali Chali Re Patang") propelled him into mainstream stardom. Throughout 1958–1968, Chitragupt crafted an effortlessly lyrical catalogue with Rajinder Krishan, Majrooh Sultanpuri, Prem Dhawan, and Sahir Ludhianvi across Barkha (1959, "Tadpaoge Tadpa Lo"), Kali Topi Lal Rumal (1959, "Laagi Chhute Naa Ab To Sanam"), Naya Sansar (1959), Patang (1960), Opera House (1961), Zabak (1961), Main Chup Rahungi (1962, "Tumhi Ho Mata Pita Tumhi Ho"), Ek Raaz (1963), Ganga Ki Laharen (1964, "Chhedo Na Meri Zulfen"), Oonche Log (1965, "Jaag Dil-E-Deewana"), and Vaasna (1968, "Yeh Parbato Ke Daayare"), while remaining active into the 1970s–1980s with Kabhi Dhoop Kabhi Chhaon (1971), Do Shikari (1979), Film Hi Film (1983), Ghar Dwaar (1985), and Shiv Ganga (1988). This dossier indexes all 134 released Hindi (Bollywood) feature films with verified playable vocal recordings and 723 tracks: strictly excluding regional Bhojpuri/Gujarati/Punjabi works (such as the landmark Bhojpuri Ganga Maiya Tohe Piyri Chadhaibo 1962 and Laagi Nahin Chhute Raam 1963), Hindi-dubbed Tamil productions (Jeevan Taara 1951 and Rangeen Duniyaa 1975), BGM-only credits (Dost 1974), non-film releases (Lata Mangeshkar - Non-film Treasures), misattributions (Chandaal 1998 by Anand–Milind; Hum Diwane 1965 by C. Ramchandra), Mahabharat’s instrumental cue, and nine 1946–1950 stunt/devotional titles lacking surviving playable uploads. Documented song origins include Ajit Merchant’s Gujarati classic "Taari Aankhno Afiini" in Naya Sansar (1959, "Chanda Loriya Sunaye"), Jim Reeves’s "Bimbo" in Opera House (1961, "Dekho Mausam Kya Bahaar Hai"), and Harry Belafonte’s "Coconut Woman" in Do Shikari (1979, "Aa Ja Gori Dil Mein Rakh Len Tujhe").',
