    editorialEssay:
      'Born in Varanasi and raised in Calcutta where he cut his first Bengali discs in 1937 before mastering Rabindra Sangeet and Adhunik Bangla songcraft, Hemanta Kumar Mukhopadhyay ("Hemant Kumar" in Bombay cinema) debuted as a Hindi film composer with Filmistan’s Anand Math (1952, immortalized by "Vande Mataram") and surged into national preeminence in 1954 through Jagriti ("Aao Bachcho Tumhe Dikhaye", "De Di Hame Aazadi"), Shart ("Na Ye Chand Hoga"), and the Filmfare Best Music Director-winning phenomenon Nagin ("Man Dole Mera Tan Dole", "Meraa Dil Ye Pukaare Aa Jaa"). Across the 1950s and 1960s he anchored beloved Hindi soundtracks including Bandish (1955), Ek Hi Raasta (1956), Inspector (1956), Champa Kali (1957), Miss Mary (1957), Fashion (1957), Sahara (1958), Chand (1959), Girl Friend (1960), Masoom (1960, where he composed "Naani Teri Morani Ko Mor Le Gae" sung by daughter Ranu Mukherjee before Robin Banerjee completed the album), Bees Saal Baad (1962, his debut as producer alongside classics "Kahin Deep Jale Kahin Dil" and "Beqaraar Karake Hamen Yun Na Jaaiye"), Guru Dutt’s Sahib Bibi Aur Ghulam (1962, "Na Jao Saiyaan Chhuda Ke Baiya", "Piya Aiso Jiya Me"), Bin Badal Barsaat (1963), Kohraa (1964, "Ye Nayan Dare Dare"), Hrishikesh Mukherjee’s Anupama (1966, "Kuchh Dil Ne Kaha", "Yaa Dil Kee Suno Dooniyawalo"), Biwi Aur Makaan (1966), Sannata (1966), Majhli Didi (1967), Do Dooni Chaar (1968), Asit Sen’s Khamoshi (1969, "Tum Pukaar Lo", "Woh Shaam Kuchh Ajeeb Thi", "Hamne Dekhi Hai"), Rahgir (1969), Us Raat Ke Baad (1970), Bees Saal Pehle (1972), Do Ladke Dono Kadke (1979), and his final Hindi score Love In Canada (1979). While his total career crossed 200 films including over 150 Bengali classics (Shap Mochan, Harano Sur, Saptapadi, Balika Bodhu), this dossier indexes strictly his 54 released Hindi (Bollywood) song feature films with 397 verified playable vocal songs: excluding all Bengali/English scores, unrealized 1969 Hindi Devi Choudhurani, 1958 track-reissue Do Mastane (whose surviving audio duplicates 1954 Daaku Ki Ladki), Hemant Bhosle misattributions (Taxi Taxi, Anpadh 1978, Sweety, Bandhan Kachchey Dhagon Ka), singer-only credits (Baat Ek Raat Ki, Jaal, Pyaasa), non-film singles and devotional/tribute LPs (Valmiki, Gaon Ki Rani), four instrumental title cues (Ferry, Sahib Bibi Aur Ghulam, Anupama, Khamoshi), and seven unuploaded 1954–1957 78 RPM tracks lacking surviving YouTube uploads. Documented origins highlight Rabindranath Tagore’s "Hey Madhabi Dwidha Keno" in Chand (1959, "Ai Baadalon, Rimajhim Ke Rang Liye"), Hemant Kumar’s own Bengali compositions "Ei Raat Tomar Amar" (Kohraa 1964, "Ye Nayan Dare Dare") and "Jadu Bhara Oi Banshi" (Do Dil 1965, "Sara Mora Kajara Chhudaya Tune"), and Christie’s 1970 hit "Yellow River" in Bees Saal Pehle (1972, "Jab Tak Ye Raat Hai Baaki").',
